1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,003
Riyadh's main international airport is closed.

2
00:00:03,003 --> 00:00:05,092
Missiles over the Saudi capital.

3
00:00:05,092 --> 00:00:08,161
Oil above one hundred and six dollars a barrel.

4
00:00:08,161 --> 00:00:13,907
And in Ukraine, a ceasefire announced by the American president is already coming apart.

5
00:00:13,907 --> 00:00:18,282
This is The World in Ten, the world explained, one story at a time.

6
00:00:18,282 --> 00:00:21,938
Today is Tuesday, October thirteenth, twenty twenty-six.

7
00:00:21,938 --> 00:00:23,832
Here's what matters, and why.

8
00:00:24,332 --> 00:00:26,318
Welcome to The World in Ten.

9
00:00:26,318 --> 00:00:30,645
This is your global briefing for Tuesday, October thirteenth.

10
00:00:30,645 --> 00:00:36,108
Today: the war in the Middle East widens as missiles strike Riyadh's airport.

11
00:00:36,108 --> 00:00:40,222
Ukraine defies Washington over a fragile energy ceasefire.

12
00:00:40,222 --> 00:00:43,131
Markets tumble across Asia as oil surges.

13
00:00:43,131 --> 00:00:49,942
The Nobel Prizes honor an economics duo and a human rights jurist, and spark a diplomatic fight.

14
00:00:49,942 --> 00:00:53,843
Artificial intelligence has its biggest week in months.

15
00:00:53,843 --> 00:00:58,880
And the clean-energy transition gets an unlikely push, from war itself.

16
00:00:58,880 --> 00:00:59,731
Let's begin.

17
00:01:00,231 --> 00:01:04,818
Our lead story: Saudi Arabia is under sustained missile and drone attack.

18
00:01:04,818 --> 00:01:12,231
On Saturday, Iran-backed Houthi rebels struck King Khalid International Airport in Riyadh, the kingdom's main air hub.

19
00:01:12,231 --> 00:01:20,147
At least thirteen people were killed, including four Saudis and an American citizen, and more than three hundred were wounded.

20
00:01:20,147 --> 00:01:26,492
Footage from inside the terminal showed passengers diving for cover as part of the ceiling collapsed.

21
00:01:26,492 --> 00:01:30,764
A second strike on Sunday killed one more person and injured eleven.

22
00:01:30,764 --> 00:01:39,057
And on Monday, Saudi air defenses intercepted three more ballistic missiles, one over Riyadh, two over the southwest of the country.

23
00:01:39,057 --> 00:01:45,465
The airport remains closed until at least October seventeenth, with several hundred flights cancelled.

24
00:01:45,465 --> 00:01:53,067
Britain's Foreign Office now advises against all but essential travel to Riyadh, Jeddah, Dammam, and most Saudi airports.

25
00:01:53,067 --> 00:01:59,098
The American aviation authority has warned U S airlines to exercise caution over Saudi airspace.

26
00:01:59,098 --> 00:02:10,972
Why it matters: this is the deadliest attack on Saudi Arabia in more than a decade, and a dramatic widening of a war that began in February, when the United States and Israel attacked Iran.

27
00:02:10,972 --> 00:02:18,951
The Houthis, armed and backed by Tehran, have now shown they can reach deep into the kingdom and shut down its busiest airport.

28
00:02:18,951 --> 00:02:24,919
What next: Saudi Arabia's energy minister has vowed what he called an equally serious response.

29
00:02:24,919 --> 00:02:37,107
President Trump says he's considering joining Saudi retaliation against the Houthis in Yemen, a decision that would deepen American involvement just three weeks before the U S midterm elections.

30
00:02:37,107 --> 00:02:40,311
We'll go deeper on this story later in the episode.

31
00:02:40,812 --> 00:02:46,006
To Ukraine, where a ceasefire announced with great fanfare is already fraying.

32
00:02:46,006 --> 00:02:57,660
On Sunday, President Trump declared what he called an immediate energy ceasefire between Russia and Ukraine, a halt to attacks on power plants, refineries, and gas facilities.

33
00:02:57,660 --> 00:03:04,852
Within hours, Ukraine's President Zelensky said Russia had violated it by striking a Ukrainian gas facility.

34
00:03:04,852 --> 00:03:16,906
And Zelensky went further: he vowed Ukraine would continue its own retaliatory strikes against Russian oil refineries, openly defying an order from the Trump administration to stop.

35
00:03:16,906 --> 00:03:21,968
Washington blames those Ukrainian strikes for record American diesel prices.

36
00:03:21,968 --> 00:03:26,030
Zelensky's answer: Kyiv will stop only if Moscow stops first.

37
00:03:26,030 --> 00:03:33,822
Analysts say that's unlikely, with winter approaching and Russia trying to cripple Ukraine's heating and electricity.

38
00:03:33,822 --> 00:03:48,672
Meanwhile, Trump announced a separate, contentious deal: Russia would supply millions of tons of diesel to the United States over the next three months, in defiance, critics say, of sanctions Trump himself signed weeks ago.

39
00:03:48,672 --> 00:03:53,134
Seven U S senators, from both parties, are demanding he reverse it.

40
00:03:53,134 --> 00:04:00,993
The volumes involved, analysts note, equal roughly twelve hours of global diesel consumption, barely a dent in prices.

41
00:04:00,993 --> 00:04:07,785
Why it matters: with American midterm elections on November third, fuel prices are political dynamite.

42
00:04:07,785 --> 00:04:19,173
But the episode reveals the limits of deal-making by announcement: a ceasefire with no agreed start date, no verification mechanism, and no sign Moscow has even responded.

43
00:04:19,173 --> 00:04:28,164
What next: watch whether Russia formally answers the ceasefire proposal, and whether Ukraine's refinery campaign continues into winter.

44
00:04:28,663 --> 00:04:30,870
Markets are feeling all of this.

45
00:04:30,870 --> 00:04:33,215
It was a risk-off day across Asia.

46
00:04:33,215 --> 00:04:36,662
South Korea's benchmark index plunged six percent.

47
00:04:36,662 --> 00:04:39,559
Japan's Nikkei fell two point six percent.

48
00:04:39,559 --> 00:04:44,730
The broad Asia-Pacific index dropped two percent, led by technology shares.

49
00:04:44,730 --> 00:04:52,936
On Wall Street overnight, the Nasdaq slipped half a percent as chipmakers sold off, while the Dow managed a small gain.

50
00:04:52,936 --> 00:04:55,901
The through-line is energy and geopolitics.

51
00:04:55,901 --> 00:05:07,693
Brent crude is near one hundred and six dollars a barrel, close to a two-week high, as the Riyadh strikes and the fragile Ukraine truce keep supply risks front and center.

52
00:05:07,693 --> 00:05:11,003
The U S dollar sits near an eighteen-month high.

53
00:05:11,003 --> 00:05:11,761
Gold eased.

54
00:05:11,761 --> 00:05:14,795
Bitcoin slipped below eighty-three thousand.

55
00:05:14,795 --> 00:05:23,898
Why it matters: as one strategist put it, markets are rediscovering that geopolitics matters most when it feeds back into inflation.

56
00:05:23,898 --> 00:05:32,586
With oil elevated, pressure stays on interest rates, which is exactly what expensive growth and A I stocks don't want to hear.

57
00:05:32,586 --> 00:05:34,586
What next: a packed calendar.

58
00:05:34,586 --> 00:05:44,516
Big U S bank earnings begin tomorrow, American inflation data lands Wednesday, and Federal Reserve Chair Kevin Warsh speaks Thursday in Bangkok.

59
00:05:44,516 --> 00:05:54,031
Finance ministers from one hundred and ninety-one countries are meeting all week in Thailand for the I M F and World Bank annual meetings.

60
00:05:54,531 --> 00:06:01,796
Now to Stockholm and Oslo, where this year's Nobel Prizes are set, and one of them has ignited a diplomatic firestorm.

61
00:06:01,796 --> 00:06:13,000
The Economics Prize went to two Americans: Robert Hall of Stanford and Michael Woodford of Columbia, honored for work on the microeconomic foundations of macroeconomic stabilization.

62
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:20,079
In plain terms: models that help central banks and governments cushion economies against shocks like energy crises.

63
00:06:20,079 --> 00:06:22,911
Their research underpins tools used worldwide.

64
00:06:22,911 --> 00:06:34,238
The Peace Prize went to Navi Pillay, the eighty-five-year-old South African jurist and former U N human rights chief, recognized for her efforts to promote peace and international law.

65
00:06:34,238 --> 00:06:41,933
Pillay served as a judge on the International Criminal Court and led the U N inquiry into the occupied Palestinian territory.

66
00:06:41,933 --> 00:06:59,047
Hours after the announcement, the Trump administration imposed sweeping financial sanctions on the International Criminal Court itself, a move the court condemned as an assault on the rule of law, and which eight allied foreign ministers, from Canada to Japan, publicly opposed.

67
00:06:59,047 --> 00:07:04,588
President Trump, who has openly campaigned for the prize, called the decision disgraceful.

68
00:07:04,588 --> 00:07:16,099
Why it matters: beyond the personalities, the prize is a statement about the rules-based order at a moment when it's under severe strain, from sanctions on courts to missiles on airports.

69
00:07:16,099 --> 00:07:19,731
What next: the prizes will be formally awarded in December.

70
00:07:20,231 --> 00:07:27,319
Artificial intelligence had one of its biggest weeks in months, for breakthroughs, warnings, and politics alike.

71
00:07:27,319 --> 00:07:41,936
Meta released two new frontier models, Muse Wonder and Muse Wonder Full, which it calls its most capable systems to date, aimed at expert work in law, finance, health, and coding, and positioned as rivals to Anthropic and Open A I.

72
00:07:41,936 --> 00:07:54,844
But the industry's mood was captured better in Singapore, where the chief executives of Broadcom and Intel warned that money, not chips, not electricity, is now the biggest constraint on the A I buildout.

73
00:07:54,844 --> 00:08:04,020
Broadcom's Hock Tan said financing could become a bigger obstacle than any hardware shortage, and compared some A I investors to casino gamblers.

74
00:08:04,020 --> 00:08:07,500
Intel's Lip-Bu Tan questioned the return on investment.

75
00:08:07,500 --> 00:08:10,474
Chip stocks fell for a fourth straight session.

76
00:08:10,474 --> 00:08:12,499
Then came the regulation debate.

77
00:08:12,499 --> 00:08:26,926
Microsoft's president, Brad Smith, told the B B C that A I now faces less regulatory oversight than washing machines or milk cartons, and called for emergency brakes, mandatory incident reporting, and independent safety vetting.

78
00:08:26,926 --> 00:08:30,850
Days earlier, Microsoft's chief executive made a similar call.

79
00:08:30,850 --> 00:08:47,871
And in Washington, President Trump tapped his national intelligence director, Jay Clayton, to lead a new federal A I task force, the Super Intelligence Force, while the Federal Trade Commission is reported to be investigating Open A I and Anthropic over consumer risks.

80
00:08:47,871 --> 00:08:51,921
Why it matters: the A I boom is entering a more skeptical phase.

81
00:08:51,921 --> 00:09:00,400
The questions are no longer just what the technology can do, but who pays for it, who governs it, and what happens when it goes wrong.

82
00:09:00,400 --> 00:09:08,184
What next: watch whether Meta's models shift the competitive balance, and whether any real regulation follows the rhetoric.

83
00:09:08,683 --> 00:09:16,157
And a striking paradox to close the briefing: the Iran war may be doing more for the climate than years of summits.

84
00:09:16,157 --> 00:09:24,476
Back in January, the International Energy Agency expected global oil demand to grow by nearly a million barrels a day this year.

85
00:09:24,476 --> 00:09:33,509
By September, it was forecasting a decline of two and a half million barrels a day instead, driven by the impasse between the U S and Iran.

86
00:09:33,509 --> 00:09:35,459
Less oil means less pollution.

87
00:09:35,459 --> 00:09:47,222
Global fossil-fuel emissions are on track to fall around half a percent in twenty twenty-six, according to Carbon Brief, the first decline since the Covid pandemic in twenty twenty.

88
00:09:47,222 --> 00:09:54,890
Electric vehicle sales rose four percent in the second quarter, as high fuel prices pushed buyers toward alternatives.

89
00:09:54,890 --> 00:09:57,360
In Europe, E V sales hit record highs.

90
00:09:57,360 --> 00:10:01,584
Why it matters: price shocks change behavior faster than pledges.

91
00:10:01,584 --> 00:10:04,964
But analysts caution that driving less is temporary.

92
00:10:04,964 --> 00:10:06,913
What lasts is what people buy.

93
00:10:06,913 --> 00:10:22,316
What next: next month's COP thirty-one climate summit in Turkey, where the E U will push to turn national climate pledges into binding action after the world officially missed the Paris goal of limiting warming to one point five degrees.

94
00:10:22,816 --> 00:10:26,380
Now, the deeper story: how did missiles reach Riyadh?

95
00:10:26,380 --> 00:10:30,416
To understand this weekend, you have to go back to February.

96
00:10:30,416 --> 00:10:45,349
That's when the United States and Israel launched a war against Iran, a conflict that sent oil above one hundred dollars and strangled the Strait of Hormuz, the waterway carrying roughly a fifth of the world's oil and gas.

97
00:10:45,349 --> 00:10:50,663
Iran's leverage was geography: close the strait, and the global economy chokes.

98
00:10:50,663 --> 00:10:53,690
Eight months later, the war has metastasized.

99
00:10:53,690 --> 00:11:02,367
The Houthis, the Iran-backed movement that controls much of Yemen, including Red Sea coastline, have become Tehran's longest arm.

100
00:11:02,367 --> 00:11:16,492
They've attacked shipping, and now they've demonstrated something new: the ability to strike Riyadh's international airport repeatedly, killing at least twenty people over the past week and forcing its closure.

101
00:11:16,492 --> 00:11:18,174
Consider what that means.

102
00:11:18,174 --> 00:11:23,218
Saudi Arabia is the world's swing oil producer and America's key Gulf ally.

103
00:11:23,218 --> 00:11:25,169
Its airports are now targets.

104
00:11:25,169 --> 00:11:27,321
Airlines are suspending flights.

105
00:11:27,321 --> 00:11:31,155
The U K is telling its citizens to stay away from Riyadh.

106
00:11:31,155 --> 00:11:40,909
At least ten massive oil tankers have stopped or reversed course near Fujairah, threatening the workaround routes that had restored Gulf exports.

107
00:11:40,909 --> 00:11:44,675
Riyadh's response tells you how seriously it takes this.

108
00:11:44,675 --> 00:11:54,832
Saudi Arabia plans to expand pipeline capacity bypassing the Strait of Hormuz by forty percent, to ten million barrels a day within two to three years.

109
00:11:54,832 --> 00:12:03,644
That's a multi-year, strategic rewiring of the world's oil map, designed for a world where Hormuz can never be fully trusted again.

110
00:12:03,644 --> 00:12:06,200
And then there's Washington's dilemma.

111
00:12:06,200 --> 00:12:10,505
Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman expects American military help.

112
00:12:10,505 --> 00:12:13,532
Saudi Arabia has bought billions in U S arms.

113
00:12:13,532 --> 00:12:21,065
But President Trump knows that deeper entanglement, three weeks before the midterms, could be politically toxic.

114
00:12:21,065 --> 00:12:25,572
American voters are already paying for this war at the diesel pump.

115
00:12:25,572 --> 00:12:27,321
Three scenarios from here.

116
00:12:27,321 --> 00:12:35,661
One: diplomacy somehow freezes the escalation, unlikely, given Tehran calls Washington's latest Hormuz proposals inadequate.

117
00:12:35,661 --> 00:12:42,320
Two: a grinding stalemate of intermittent strikes, with airports and shipping under rolling threat.

118
00:12:42,320 --> 00:12:49,787
Three: full escalation, with the U S joining Saudi strikes on Yemen, and Iran answering against Gulf neighbors.

119
00:12:49,787 --> 00:12:53,823
The through-line: this is no longer a war contained to Iran.

120
00:12:53,823 --> 00:12:59,944
It's a regional conflict redrawing energy routes, airline maps, and alliances in real time.

121
00:13:00,443 --> 00:13:01,697
What to watch next.

122
00:13:01,697 --> 00:13:10,073
Tomorrow: America's biggest banks report earnings, the unofficial start of earnings season, and a health check on the consumer.

123
00:13:10,073 --> 00:13:12,051
Wednesday: U S inflation data.

124
00:13:12,051 --> 00:13:19,965
With oil above one-oh-six and diesel at record highs, this number matters for the Federal Reserve, and for the midterms.

125
00:13:19,965 --> 00:13:33,090
Thursday: Fed Chair Kevin Warsh speaks in Bangkok, on the sidelines of the I M F and World Bank meetings, where one hundred and ninety-one countries are debating A I, debt, and geopolitical upheaval.

126
00:13:33,090 --> 00:13:41,334
Next month: the COP thirty-one climate summit in Turkey, where the E U will push to turn climate pledges into binding action.

127
00:13:41,334 --> 00:13:50,435
And November third: America's midterm elections, with diesel prices, the Iran war, and now Riyadh all on the ballot in one way or another.

128
00:13:50,936 --> 00:13:55,302
The takeaway: the world's crises are no longer distant headlines.

129
00:13:55,302 --> 00:13:59,199
A missile over Riyadh moves the price at your gas station.

130
00:13:59,199 --> 00:14:03,095
A ceasefire announced on social media unravels by morning.

131
00:14:03,095 --> 00:14:10,350
And the institutions built to keep the peace, courts, treaties, central banks, are being tested all at once.

132
00:14:10,350 --> 00:14:11,895
That's the world today.

133
00:14:11,895 --> 00:14:19,621
If this briefing helped you understand it, subscribe to The World in Ten, the world explained, one story at a time.

134
00:14:19,621 --> 00:14:21,704
We'll see you tomorrow at nine.

